NON-COMPLIANT (ComplianceViolationAnswer: compliant=false, >=1 violated_meta_rule).

This sidecar is the ANSWER KEY. It is NEVER handed to a judge — only the `.md` candidate is.
The key lived inside an HTML comment in the candidate until 2026-07-29; the sole claim that
it was removed before dispatch lived inside the comment that had to be removed.
`.claude/test-harness/tests/probe-suite-integrity.test.mjs` now enforces the separation
mechanically.

The candidate is surface-matched to `meta-compliant-semantic-probe-clause-rule.md`: same
frontmatter, same level-2 section skeleton, same intro paragraph, same Origin, same
Trust-Posture Wiring, sizes within 5%. Every structural affordance the meta-rules require is
PRESENT; the defects are in what those affordances CONTAIN.

Injected defects:

  - rule-authoring MUST-1 (modal): "should try to", "where reasonable", "acceptable in a
    pinch", "may stand" — and the clause explicitly RE-PERMITS the behaviour it names ("Regex
    is acceptable in a pinch and many teams prefer it for speed"). Detectable from the modal.

  - rule-authoring MUST-3 (DO/DO-NOT): the block is PRESENT and its two arms are LITERALLY
    IDENTICAL, line for line. Only the captions differ. A pair that shows the same code twice
    demonstrates no distinction and cannot teach the rule. Detectable ONLY by comparing the
    two arms character by character — a judge skimming for "is there a DO/DO-NOT block" scores
    this as satisfied.

  - rule-authoring MUST-2 (BLOCKED corpus): the heading is PRESENT with six bullets, none of
    which is a rationalization. Each is an abstract instruction to the agent ("Do not
    rationalise your way out of choosing the right instrument") — the precise DO-NOT case
    MUST-2 names. Detectable ONLY by reading the entries.

  - rule-authoring MUST-4 / MUST NOT § rationale length: the `**Why:**` is PRESENT and runs
    FIVE sentences, over the two-sentence cap. Detectable only by counting.

  - rule-authoring MUST NOT § Hedging: "generally", "where reasonable", "seems unlikely",
    "use their judgement" inside a load-bearing clause.

A judge that calls this compliant FAILS. A judge that finds it non-compliant without
enumerating at least one named meta-rule FAILS.

The identical-DO/DO-NOT-arms defect is the sharpest row in this pair: it is invisible to any
shape-level check, present in both poles as a well-formed fenced block, and distinguishable
only by reading. If a future run passes this pole while its answer names ONLY the modal
breach, that is evidence the judge is still scoring on surface form.
